+**The audio-integrity run (`audio.integrity`).**
+Its [signal design](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/396#issuecomment-5978644444) is a bit-exact counter on every digital path.
+`release_audio_integrity` runs concurrently with `soak.continuous`, inside its window.
+It starts after the soak binds every pair and starts timing.
+It ends at or before the soak endpoint and lasts at least 86400 seconds.
+Those seconds are qualified, observed capture, defined below.
+So each capture runs past them at both ends by its guard samples and by any logged loss.
+The soak window outlasts 24 hours by as much.
+Every channel of every bound AAF stream carries the counter, in both directions.
+The DUT talker reaches an external digital capture through the reference peer.
+The reference peer feeds the DUT listener, its TDM output and the direct TDM capture (#451).
+The DUT's TDM loop is graded as well.
+CRF streams carry no audio samples, so no counter rides on them.
+
+Each word is S32, with the 24-bit sample in bits 31:8 and bits 7:0 zero.
+The #451 and #629 captures use the same layout.
+
+| Sample bits | Content |
+|---|---|
+| 15:12 | The channel number, the channel index plus one |
+| 11:1 | The sample's absolute position `n`, modulo 2048 |
+| 0 | Bit `n mod 32` of block `n div 2048`'s code: the block number times `0x9E3779B1`, modulo 2^32 |
+
+No valid word is zero, so silence stays distinct from data.
+A corruption can still make a word zero, and that word reads as silence.
+Thirty-two consecutive samples of one block give the block number.
+So the counter repeats only after 2^43 samples.
+That is about 530 days at 192 kHz, the highest rate in Milan v1.2 6.2 (Table 6.1).
+A 24-hour run is 1.66e10 samples at that rate, and 4.15e9 at 48 kHz.
+Any 64 clean samples fix a channel's absolute position.
+Eight channels peak near -47 dBFS.
+The 2048-sample wrap is a 23.4 Hz sawtooth at 48 kHz, so keep it off loudspeakers.
+
+`grade_audio_counter_capture` grades one headerless interleaved S32 capture.
+State its channel count, byte order and rate, as `tb/tools/thdn.py` callers do.
+`tb/tools/thdn.py` documents the external capture as big-endian.
+The #451 direct TDM capture recorded little-endian words.
+Supply the capture as bytes or as an iterable of chunks, so a 24-hour file streams.
+`tags` names each channel's number; `None` marks a channel without the counter.
+Each channel is graded on its own.
+A slip on one channel, or a channel without the counter, leaves the rest on the fast path.
+Each channel's number and position are acquired from its first 64 clean samples.
+A mismatch opens a window that closes at the next 64 clean samples.
+Their decoded position sizes every slip inside the window exactly.
+Each event is classified at its capture index:
+
+| Class | Signature |
+|---|---|
+| `dropped`, `n` | The position resumes `n` samples ahead |
+| `repeated`, `n` | The position resumes `n` samples behind |
+| `silence`, `n` | `n` zero words; `inserted` when the content resumes where it stopped |
+| `bit-flip`, `n`, `mask` | `n` corrupted words, with the OR of their XOR masks against the expected words |
+| `swap`, `n`, `partner` | `n` words exchanged both ways with the partner channel |
+| `unresolved`, `n`, `mask` | At either end of a capture, `n` words that differ from the track only in the code bit |
+
+An `unresolved` event is a slip of whole blocks or a code-bit corruption; the capture cannot tell which.
+Its `located_within` bounds the sample the slip would be at.
+It is a DUT event, so it fails the run, but it never claims an exact class.
+
+These rules decide, in order, between patterns that look alike:
+
+1. The channel number decides first.
+   Only a reciprocal exchange is a `swap`.
+   A flipped channel number, or another channel's word copied in, is a `bit-flip`.
+   Its `matches_channel` names the channel whose word it equals.
+2. A channel swapped from the first frame is a `swap` over the whole run.
+   Its counter is still tracked, so its planted arms are still found.
+3. One word that leaves the track and returns at once is a `bit-flip`.
+   The exception is a word equal to a neighbouring received word, which is a repeat.
+   A one-sample hold reads as 1 repeated and then 1 dropped.
+   A skip followed by a hold reads as 1 dropped and then 1 repeated.
+4. Two or more words that leave the track and return, with one XOR mask against it, are one `bit-flip`, at any length.
+   The exception is a mask that clears the channel number, which is outside the exactness scope below.
+   Each word it makes zero reads as `silence` and splits the excursion.
+   A word at either end under another mask is its own one-word `bit-flip` when no single offset explains the excursion.
+   So is each word of a two-word excursion under two masks that no single offset explains.
+   Otherwise each change of position is its own slip.
+
+Rules 3 and 4 judge each departure against the offset in force when it leaves.
+They judge again after every slip, so a slip and a corruption in one window are both graded, in either order.
+That holds inside the exactness scope below, except for the planted-arm voids it states.
+A word that no offset explains is cut out of its run of positions.
+The offset in force before a change stays a candidate after it.
+So a corrupted word that happens to fit the next track never strands the words after it.
+Each slip takes the smallest size the positions allow.
+A slip of whole blocks is placed at the first word that shows it.
+A departure ends at the first word from which every word up to its return fits the offset in force.
+It also ends at the first word that fits the offset in force after a leading stretch under one mask.
+That holds when no single offset of whole blocks explains the stretch; the coincident words after it are its return.
+An excursion under one mask that ends in a slip is one `bit-flip` and that slip.
+That holds when no single offset explains its words and none is on the slip's track.
+As slips it would be three events or more.
+A slip of whole blocks has a `located_within` that runs back over the words that fit both tracks.
+It runs on to the first word that shows the slip.
+A corrupted word bounds it only where the two tracks differ; elsewhere its mask is the same on either side.
+
+**Every reading balances.**
+On every channel, the slips a reading reports sum to the shift its closing 64 samples decode.
+Inserted silence counts as a slip back.
+A reading that does not balance is never taken while one that does exists.
+A held excursion's slips count until it returns, when its `bit-flip` replaces them.
+The grader keeps that ledger per channel and checks it at every window after acquisition.
+A window no reading balances is one `unresolved` event at its first sample.
+Its reason reads "an unclassified corruption: no reading's slips sum to the shift the bytes decode".
+So no unbalanced slip is ever reported.
+Every reading balances by construction, so this is the guard behind it.
+
+Where the bytes allow two readings, the planted-arm log decides at a logged arm.
+Of the readings it tries, the grader takes another only when it has fewer DUT events, or as many and more arms found.
+So the log never adds a DUT event, and an arm the capture does not show is still missed.
+It does not try every reading the bytes allow.
+So outside the exactness scope, a DUT event over a planted arm can void the run although a reading of the bytes finds the arm.
+Inside the scope, the voids below are the only shapes that miss an arm, and a void blocks the release.
+A DUT slip that undoes a planted slip arm at the next sample leaves one displaced word.
+The log reads that word as the arm and the DUT slip, not as a corruption.
+Arms may be planted closer than 64 samples.
+A DUT slip beside an arm, at another sample, fails the run with every arm found.
+
+Sixty-four corrupted words under one mask can decode as another track.
+That track is the old one corrupted, so the grader holds it instead of adopting it.
+Only the window's trailing words under that mask start the hold, so an event before them is graded on its own.
+The old one is the track at the offset in force where those words begin.
+That is the window's opening offset or one the reading reaches, whichever leaves the longest masked run.
+So a slip before the excursion sizes the hold.
+What is recorded before the hold must end on that offset, as the bytes do.
+A reading that does not end there is read again with the masked words restored.
+The held slips are then the ones the masked words read as from that offset.
+A hold is made only where a slip of the reading may lie in the masked words.
+A word that is the old track's own is the return, never a release.
+If the words return to the old track, the whole excursion is one `bit-flip`.
+It ends where the old track's words began, and the rest of the window is graded against the old track.
+So a one-word event beside the excursion, at either end, is graded on its own.
+Content that resumes nearer the old track than the held one has returned; a slip after the return is its own event.
+If a word leaves the mask first, or a masked word follows the old track's own, the held events are released as the slips they are.
+
+**The qualified interval.**
+A slip of whole blocks changes only the code bit, so it shows only within 63 samples.
+Any 63 consecutive samples hold one aligned 32-sample code group, so it always shows within them.
+So a verdict proves a sample clean only when the 63 samples before it lie on the acquired track and the 63 after it were captured.
+Every verdict states that interval in `qualified`.
+It runs from 63 samples after the last counter channel's acquisition to 63 samples before the capture's end.
+Nothing outside it is proved clean, so a slip there may pass unseen.
+The interval decides what is proved clean, not which class a slip near its end receives.
+A slip of whole blocks with fewer than 64 captured samples after its first differing word reads as `unresolved`.
+That holds inside the interval too.
+Two captures can be byte-identical while one hides such a slip in its last samples.
+`qualified` also states the interval's frames, seconds and acquisition indices, and the logged losses inside it.
+A logged loss is time that ran, but nothing in it was observed, so it does not count.
+`grade_audio_counter_capture` takes `required_s`, 86400 seconds by default.
+`PASS` needs zero DUT events and at least `required_s` observed seconds in the interval.
+A shorter interval is `NOT RUN`, so a run without its guards cannot qualify; an observed event still fails.
+
+Some patterns are bit-identical, so the signal cannot tell them apart.
+These limits are stated, and a self-test arm pins each one:
+
+- A flip of position bit 0 can make a word equal to its neighbour; it then reads as the slip pair it duplicates.
+- A slip pair whose displaced words share one XOR mask with the track reads as a `bit-flip`, at any length.
+  A repeat and a later drop of half a block, inside one region of that block, is one example.
+  Where the pair's total is whole blocks, it reads as that `bit-flip` and a slip of whole blocks beside it.
+  The corruption is judged against whichever track gives its mask fewer bits set, the slip first when that is the new track.
+- A slip of whole 2048-sample blocks leaves bits 11:1 unchanged.
+  It is found within 63 samples and sized exactly.
+  `located_within` gives the samples it may have happened at.
+- A slip whose track equals the old one under one mask is held until the masks part.
+  Half a block inside a region of a block, and the blocks that invert the code, are such slips.
+  The masks part at most one block later, and the slip is then sized exactly.
+- Between events less than 64 samples apart, each slip takes the smallest size the positions allow.
+  A code bit those sizes leave unexplained then reads as a code-bit `bit-flip`.
+  The cluster's total is exact.
+- A code-bit corruption less than 64 samples before a slip of whole blocks may make its word the new track's.
+  When every word between them fits both tracks, the bytes are that slip alone at the corrupted word, and read so.
+- A slip of whole blocks and a code-bit corruption less than 64 samples apart can give the same bytes in either order.
+  That happens when the corrupted word is the other track's and only one word between them differs.
+  The corruption then reads first, and the slip is located after it.
+- A slip followed, within the words that fit both tracks, by a slip of whole blocks gives the bytes of one slip of their sum.
+  It reads as that one slip.
+- At a logged arm, the planted-arm log decides between such readings, as above.
+  A DUT slip at a planted slip arm's own sample is one slip of their sum.
+  Unless the DUT slip is whole blocks, the arm is then missed and the run is void.
+- A one-word corruption on a planted slip arm's own sample, or the next one, may read first.
+  It is then judged against the offset before the slip, and the slip is placed after it, within the arm tolerance.
+  The bytes are the same either way.
+- Beside a planted flip arm, on its channel, under a slip of whole blocks, the arm's word is the other track's where the two differ.
+  So the bytes may be that slip on the other side of the arm, with the arm's code bit one sample over.
+  Within the tolerance the arm is found, and `located_within` bounds the slip of that reading.
+- A constant-mask corruption that a slip interrupts reads as the slips its words show.
+  So does one whose words a single offset explains up to a slip at its return.
+  Where an interrupted one's words before the slip are held as one mask, they read as one `bit-flip` up to the slip.
+- At either end of a capture, fewer than 64 clean samples lie beyond a word whose code bit differs.
+  So a slip of whole blocks and a code-bit corruption read the same there, and the stretch is one `unresolved` event.
+  At the capture's end it runs from the first such word to the end.
+  Before a channel's acquisition it runs to the last such word.
+- Any other slip that 64 clean samples cannot confirm carries `unresolved`, with the smallest size its positions allow.
+  That holds in a capture's last samples, and for a held excursion that the capture ends inside.
+- A stretch longer than 65536 words without 64 clean samples is graded in pieces.
+  A corruption with one mask stays a `bit-flip` with that mask in each piece; a slip in a piece carries `unresolved`.
+
+Inside the exactness scope, two shapes beside a planted arm void the run, so the release cannot qualify.
+Each event is still listed:
+
+- A corrupted word on a planted slip arm's own sample, or on the one before it, that is another position's word.
+  That is its channel's word at a position at most half a block (1024 samples) from the old or the new track's.
+  It reads as a slip to that position and on to the arm's track, so the arm is missed.
+  A mask that moves sample bits 11:1, with or without the code bit, can do this.
+  For one corrupted word, the code bit alone, a channel-number bit or a pad bit cannot.
+- A corruption of a planted flip arm's own word, on its channel, combines with the arm's mask.
+  One beside it under the arm's own mask makes a two-word excursion of that mask.
+  Either way the arm is missed.
+
+`test_audio_composition_sweep` grades every placement of these one-word shapes.
+It covers a one-word corruption 0 to 4 samples before or after each planted arm, on every channel.
+It uses eight mask classes, two capture lengths and the release tolerance of 2.
+Each case is checked against the class its bytes put it in: exact, a stated limit, or one of the voids.
+`test_audio_slip_before_a_constant_mask` grades a short slip 1 to 64 samples before a constant-mask excursion.
+Its excursions run 64 to 2049 words at three block positions, under five masks.
+
+**The property and its exactness scope.**
+Every capture meets one property, whatever its events.
+A capture with a DUT event the bytes show never grades `PASS`.
+Each such event is covered, on each channel it changes, by at least one listing.
+A listing covers an event when its samples overlap the event's first word minus 63 to its last word plus 63.
+A listing's samples are its words, and for a slip the samples `located_within` names as well.
+The 63 is the furthest a slip of whole blocks can lie before the first word that shows it.
+A DUT event inside a constant-mask excursion on the same channel may be covered by that excursion's listing alone.
+The one exception is a DUT event that masks a planted arm at its sample.
+The run is then void, and its reason says the source may not have planted the arm, or a DUT event may have masked it.
+Events that cancel one another, or only move an arm within its tolerance, leave a clean run's bytes.
+The capture does not show them.
+
+Inside the exactness scope below, each listing begins within 63 samples of the event's first word.
+A listing begins at the sample it names, its `capture_index`; a slip's `located_within` can start before it.
+Outside the scope, the property promises coverage, not position.
+
+`test_audio_compositions_never_pass_and_list_every_event` is the gate for the property.
+It grades 288 seeded compositions of one to four DUT events over and beside each of the six planted arms.
+The events are slips of 1 to 4096 samples, silences, swaps, copies, one-word corruptions and constant-mask excursions.
+The excursions run up to 2100 words.
+Their masks are the sweep's eight classes, the mask that clears the channel's number, and any other word.
+It grades 48 more on each of the two capture lengths, with DUT events inside an excursion.
+Each is a constant-mask excursion of 64 to 2100 words anywhere, under one of the sweep's mask classes.
+One or two DUT events lie inside it, slips in three draws of seven, and half of them less than 64 samples before its return.
+It checks the property, not a class: each event of each composition is covered on each channel it changes.
+Where a run fails without voiding, it also checks that each DUT listing covers an event that changes its channel.
+In a void run, a missed arm's own records are DUT listings, so that check does not apply there.
+Each allowance it makes is pinned once: an arm moved within its tolerance, events that cancel, and an arm masked at its sample.
+
+Exact classification, as the rules and limits above state, is claimed only for these shapes:
+
+- One DUT event with at least 64 clean samples between it and every other event and planted arm.
+  An excursion whose mask clears its channel number is the exception.
+- A one-word corruption 0 to 4 samples from a planted arm, as `test_audio_composition_sweep` grades it.
+- A short slip 1 to 64 samples before a constant-mask excursion, as `test_audio_slip_before_a_constant_mask` grades it.
+  The excursion's mask makes no channel number zero or another channel's.
+
+Any other composition grades `FAIL` or void, and may read in other classes.
+Three such shapes are pinned, so a change to their reading is seen:
+
+- A constant-mask excursion over a planted slip arm's sample may void the run, although a reading of the bytes finds the arm.
+  `test_audio_excursion_over_a_slip_arm_may_void` pins one: `0x100` over five words from the repeat arm's sample.
+- An excursion whose mask clears its channel number splits at each word it makes zero.
+  That word is a block start whose code bit is 0, and it reads as one sample of `silence` between two `bit-flip` events.
+  `test_audio_excursion_clearing_its_channel_number_is_split` pins one: 65 words under `0x100000` on channel 0.
+- A constant-mask excursion that a DUT slip interrupts, away from the arms, fails, each event covered.
+  `test_audio_excursion_a_slip_interrupts_keeps_its_bit_flip` pins one.
+  It is `0x80100` over channel 0's words 4424 to 6149, with a DUT repeat of 3 at 6097 on every channel.
+  Its words before the slip read as one `bit-flip` up to it.
+
+The planted flip arm's mask `0x00000100` flips the code bit alone.
+The word keeps its position bits, so it never equals a neighbour.
+
+Start each capture once the counter flows on every channel.
+Words before a channel's acquisition are graded against the track it acquires, so leading silence fails.
+A channel that has not acquired the counter within its first 65536 frames voids the run at once.
+That is usually a wrong layout or byte order, so the grader stops instead of reading the rest.
+It may also be a channel the DUT never drives with the counter; the reason names both.
+A channel that never acquires the counter, so has no qualified interval, is void for the same reasons.
+
+Each event records its class, capture and acquisition indices, expected and received words, and times.
+The capture tool records anchors: a capture index with its wall and gPTP times.
+Record one at least every `audio_context_s` and one at the capture's end.
